Everything under this directory came from Omarchy and is not ours.

    https://github.com/basecamp/omarchy
    MIT

None of it is committed. `download-deps.sh` fetches it at the pinned tag, so
this tree holds no copy of somebody else's text.

**What is committed is the difference.** `patches/` holds one patch for each
file we changed, and `download-deps.sh` applies them after the copy. Editing a
file here is fine; leaving the edit only here is not, because the next fetch
overwrites it. Make the patch:

    ./DE-shell/vendor/patches/make.sh omarchy-pkg-install

A patch is less to carry than a fork of the file, and it fails loudly. When
upstream moves the lines under it, `git apply` refuses and names the patch,
which is the moment to look at what changed. A copy would have swallowed it.

`MANIFEST` holds the sha256 of each file **as upstream wrote it**, before any
patch, and `check.sh` compares those against the omarchy installed on this
machine. So drift detection still works on a patched file: the manifest never
describes our copy.

`pkg/` is the package tools. Four answer a question about what is installed,
three are fzf panels, and two are the helpers the panels source. They go to
/usr/bin under their own names, because they call each other by name and
because renaming them would turn every upstream fix into a merge.

`setters/` is run when a theme is applied, one script for each application we
do not ship. `helpers/` is on their PATH and is never run directly.

Every one of them reads $HOME/.local/state/omarchy/current/theme. fig
links that path to its own theme directory rather than patching fifteen
files.

